title: 'eth-rpc: keep the sync head behind un-indexed subscription gaps'
doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    The subscription gap filler no longer lets the `sync-head` label advance over blocks it failed to index. Previously a gap fill that errored, or a gap dropped because the gap queue was full, still counted as done, so the head moved past the missing blocks and a restart never filled them. `eth_getLogs` over a block range then omitted those blocks' logs, `eth_getLogs` by such a block's Ethereum block hash reported the block as unknown, and the lookups served from the index returned `null` or `0` for them: `eth_getTransactionReceipt`, `eth_getTransactionByHash`, `eth_getTransactionByBlockHashAndIndex`, `eth_getTransactionByBlockNumberAndIndex`, `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByHash`, `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByNumber`, and every method addressed by such a block's Ethereum block hash, whose mapping to the substrate block was never written. Methods that read the node, such as `eth_getBlockReceipts` by number and `eth_getTransactionCount`, were not affected. A finalized block whose own processing failed was skipped the same way; it is now handed to the gap filler like a missed block. Blocks finalized between the start of the backward sync and the start of the finalized subscription could stay unindexed the same way; they are now queued like a missed range. A block the gap filler indexes does not evict a retracted sibling at the same height that was indexed while it was the best block; both stay in the index until a resync. Such a block is also missing from `eth_feeHistory`, and its logs are not pushed to `eth_subscribe` log subscriptions.

    A failed gap fill is now attempted up to 5 times, waiting 2, 4, 8 and then 16 seconds between attempts, about 30 seconds of waiting plus the attempts themselves, each of which walks the range again; an outage that outlasts them leaves the gap to the restart described below. In archive mode (`--eth-pruning=archive`) a gap that still fails, or that did not fit in the queue, keeps the sync head at its current block for the rest of the process lifetime. Later blocks are still indexed and served, but the index-backed methods listed above stay incomplete for the gap's blocks until the next restart, whose backward sync walks from the frozen head up to the current finalized block and extracts the blocks that are not yet indexed. Operators who see the error "in archive mode the sync head stays at its current block until a restart fills the gap" should restart eth-rpc. A restart fills the gap only once its cause has cleared. If the log shows `Sync stopped due to` after the restart, the gap could not be extracted and the sync head stays at its block; a backfill of older history that is still running continues regardless. When the connected node no longer has the gap's state, point eth-rpc at an archive node and restart again; deleting the database and resyncing only helps with such a node. When the same block fails on every restart against an archive node, the failure is in eth-rpc itself: upgrade it and report the block. With `--eth-pruning <N>` there is no sync head and the index is in memory, so a restart does not help: the gap's blocks stay unindexed until they leave the retained window. Handing a finalized block to the gap filler because its processing failed, and queuing the blocks between the backward sync's start and the subscription's start, apply to archive mode only; with `--eth-pruning <N>` such blocks are logged or left unindexed, as before.

    The fix prevents new holes only. An archive database that already lost a gap under the previous behaviour has the hole below its sync head, and nothing revisits it. The earlier versions left no trace of it in the database, only in their logs: `Subscription gap fill failed for`, `Subscription gap queue error, dropping`, `Failed to process block` or `Failed to resolve streamed`, each naming the block or range. The last two also come from the best-block stream, whose blocks are indexed again once finalized; they mark a hole when logged for a finalized block. A database whose logs show such a line has to be deleted and resynced to repair it; without them no action is needed. No configuration changes are needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: patch
